      <EssayHeader slug="skin-in-the-game" />

      <p class="e-thesis"><span class="e-thesis-label">In short:</span> The standard promise that a fund manager will invest two percent of his own money to “align interests” began as a tax rule, is often not funded with the manager’s own cash, and matters far less than the manager’s cut of the profits.</p>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">There is a sentence in every private fund document that gets read aloud more often than any other and read silently almost never. It sits in the summary of terms, somewhere after the management fee and before the key person clause. With small variations it says that the General Partner will commit an amount equal to two percent of aggregate Commitments to the Fund, in order to ensure an alignment of interests between the General Partner and the Limited Partners. (The General Partner, or GP, is the firm that manages the fund. The Limited Partners, or LPs, are the investors.)</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Then the phrase takes on a life of its own. It appears on the third slide of the deck. It comes up in the first meeting, unprompted, usually with a hand placed briefly on the chest. It answers a question the investor has not yet asked, and it is offered early precisely so the question is never asked in the form that would matter.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">I have sat on both sides of it. For a while my job was to read that sentence in other people’s documents on behalf of a family that was being asked to sign. Later my job was to help write it. What strikes me now is not that the sentence is false. It is not false, and the money does go in. What strikes me is that everyone in the room, on both sides of the table, treats a number as a virtue. Nobody in fifty years has felt the need to say where the number came from, who is actually paying it, or what it would have to look like to do the thing it claims to do.</p>
        <p>So let us take it apart. It has three components, and like most sentences that survive unread for decades, each is doing something other than what it says.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="my-14">
