      <h2 class="e-h2">Where One Percent Came From</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">Start with the number, because it is the part everyone assumes was chosen. For most of the industry’s history it was one percent. Now it is more often two, sometimes three, and the placement agent will tell you that five is what the best houses do. Ask why the convention settled at one and you will get a story about custom, or what the market bears, or a shrug. Here is the actual answer.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">In 1989 the Internal Revenue Service issued a revenue procedure setting out when it would treat a limited partnership as a partnership rather than a corporation for tax purposes. One condition was that the general partners, together, hold at least a one percent interest in each material item of the partnership’s income, gain, loss, deduction and credit (for larger funds the required percentage could scale down, to as little as 0.2 percent for funds above $50 million). This was not for alignment. It was for classification. A general partner with less than one percent looked, to the IRS, too much like a manager and not enough like a partner, and the whole vehicle risked being taxed as a corporation. So every fund formed in the following decade put one percent of the general partner’s own money in, because the lawyers said to, and the lawyers said to largely because of a tax safe harbor.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">In late 1996 the Treasury adopted its “check-the-box” regulations, effective January 1, 1997, and the classification question went away. An entity could now choose its tax treatment on a form. The safe harbor lost its purpose. The one percent stayed. It stayed because by then it was in every template document, every fund lawyer had drafted it a hundred times, and every investor had been told, and had come to believe, that it was there for them. Within a few years the phrase about alignment of interests had been attached to it. A compliance floor put in place to satisfy a revenue agent had become a moral commitment made to a pension fund.</p>
        <p>I raise this not to say the commitment is a fraud. I raise it because the industry tells you the number is a measure of conviction, and the number was never a measure of anything. It was a threshold. When the threshold was retired, nobody recalculated. They kept the figure and changed the story about what it meant, and the story is what gets read aloud in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Who Writes the Check</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The second component is the verb: the General Partner “will commit.” The passive-sounding grammar hides a question that any investor would ask of any other counterparty in any other transaction, and that limited partners as a class almost never ask here: with what?</p>
        <p class="mb-6">There are three honest answers, and the document rarely says which one applies.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>1. Cash.</strong> The money comes from the personal bank accounts of the individuals who run the firm, wired in alongside the investors’ capital on the same day. That is what the sentence implies, and what the hand on the chest is meant to convey.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>2. A management fee waiver.</strong> The manager chooses to forgo some of the fee it would otherwise be paid, and in exchange receives an interest in the fund’s profits that the documents treat as though the waived amount had been contributed as capital. No cash moves. The commitment is funded out of the fee, which is paid by the investors. So the investors are financing the manager’s alignment with the investors, and being told it is a sign of the manager’s conviction. The Treasury took an interest in this arrangement in 2015, for its own reasons, which had to do with converting ordinary income into deferred capital gain, and proposed rules to curb the more aggressive versions. The practice did not stop. It moved into the footnotes.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>3. Borrowed money.</strong> A bank lends against the management fee stream, which is contractual and lasts ten years, or against the expected carried interest (the manager’s share of profits), and the loan funds the commitment. Or, increasingly, a stake in the management company is sold to a specialist buyer, and the proceeds fund the commitment to the next fund. Look at the use-of-proceeds paragraph in many “GP stakes” transactions and you will find language like this in plain sight: to fund general partner commitments to future funds. The firm has sold a piece of its future fee income to a third party so that it can tell its investors it is putting its own money in. Nothing in the fund documents changes. The sentence about alignment reads exactly as it did.</p>
        <p>None of these three arrangements is hidden, exactly. All are disclosed somewhere, if you know the paragraph to look for. But the sentence in the summary of terms does not distinguish between them, the slide does not, and the meeting does not. The difference between a partner wiring two percent of a fund from his own account and a firm borrowing it against fees the same investors will pay is not a difference of degree. It is the difference between having skin in the game and having a photograph of some.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">The Arithmetic of Alignment</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">The third component is the noun, “alignment.” Here it is worth doing something almost nobody in the room does, which is the arithmetic.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Take a fund of a billion dollars.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The commitment:</strong> two percent is twenty million dollars from the general partner, if it is cash, which we have seen it may not be.</li>
          <li><strong>The management fee:</strong> at the customary rate, twenty million a year during the investment period, stepping down afterward. Call it a hundred and fifty million over the life of the fund in round numbers, more if there are successor funds that also pay fees.</li>
          <li><strong>The carried interest:</strong> twenty percent of profits above a hurdle. If the fund doubles, the carry is on the order of two hundred million.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mb-6">Now set the general partner’s exposures side by side. On the downside, if the fund goes to zero, the general partner loses twenty million and has been paid a hundred and fifty million in fees for the privilege. On the upside, if the fund doubles, the general partner makes twenty million on its capital and two hundred million on its carry. The commitment is not the alignment. The commitment is a rounding error on the alignment. Whatever overlap of interest exists between the manager and the investor comes almost entirely from the carry, and the carry has the shape of an option: all of the upside above a line, none of the downside below it. The two percent is a small premium, paid or waived or borrowed, that makes the option look like a share.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">There is a way to say this that is fair to the manager. Fund managers do not have to put money into their funds at all. The commitment is a real cost, especially for the younger partners at a firm. They are often required to take up their allocated share and often borrow from the firm to do so, at an age and net worth where two percent of a large fund is a very large number for them. If you want to find the people whose skin is actually in the game, look there. Do not look at the founder, whose share of the commitment is a fraction of a fraction of his liquid net worth. Look at the thirty-four-year-old principal with a loan against a bonus that has not been paid yet. She is aligned. She is also not the person making the investment decisions.</p>
        <p>That is the quiet inversion at the center of the sentence. Alignment is a relationship between the person deciding and the person bearing the consequences. The commitment, as usually structured, does the opposite. It concentrates the consequences on the people with the least say and dilutes them for the people with the most, and then reports the total to the investor as a single percentage, as if the general partner were one person.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Where This Breaks</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The industry has noticed.</strong> The institutional investors’ trade body has said for years that the commitment should be substantial, funded in cash rather than through fee waivers, and disclosed by source. The better LPs now ask exactly the questions this essay says are never asked. Some firms, particularly ones still run by their founders, put in five or ten percent from personal accounts and say so, and for them the sentence means what it says. First-time funds, which have no track record to sell, use a large cash commitment as the only credible signal they have, and there it carries real information. All of that is true and none of it is contradicted here. The claim is not that the commitment is always a fiction. The claim is that the sentence is written the same way whether it is a fiction or not, and the way it is read aloud in the room is designed to make the distinction disappear.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>The remedy favors the rich.</strong> Suppose you took the essay’s logic to its end and demanded a commitment scaled to what would really hurt, some meaningful fraction of each partner’s liquid net worth. You would have built a rule that only the already rich can satisfy. A talented manager without inherited capital could not raise a fund. You would have entrenched the incumbents in the name of aligning them, and the incumbents, who can afford it, would be the first to endorse the rule. That is a serious objection and I do not have a clean answer to it. I would only note that it is an objection to a remedy, not to the diagnosis, and that a rule which cannot be fixed cleanly is a poor reason to keep reading a number aloud as though it were a virtue.</p>
        <p><strong>The carry is the alignment, and everyone knows it.</strong> The third objection is that the commitment was always understood as ceremony. Perhaps. But if it is ceremony, then say so, and stop putting the phrase about alignment of interests after it. A firm that describes its two percent as customary is being accurate. A firm that describes it as ensuring alignment is making a claim, and a claim can be checked. The checking is the whole point.</p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="e-h2">Three Questions</h2>
      <div class="e-body">
        <p class="mb-6">What follows is small, on purpose. It is not a reform of fund terms, which is beyond any one investor. It is three questions that fit on an index card and that change what the sentence is worth the moment they are asked.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>How is it funded?</strong> Cash, waiver or borrowed, and in what proportions. This is a factual question with a factual answer, and a manager who cannot or will not give it in one sentence has answered it. Insist on the split. A commitment that is sixty percent fee waiver is a commitment of forty percent of the number on the slide, and the investor is entitled to know which number he is being asked to admire.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>Whose money is it?</strong> Not the general partner as an entity. The people. What share of the commitment is borne by the founders, what share by the investment partners who sit on the committee, what share by the people below them, and is any of it lent by the firm to the individuals who owe it? This is the question that exposes the inversion. If most of the commitment sits with the people who make the decisions, the sentence is doing what it says. If most of it sits with the people who carry them out, it is not, and the honest description would be that the firm has arranged for its junior staff to be aligned on the senior staff’s behalf.</p>
        <p class="mb-6"><strong>What is it, to them?</strong> This is the one nobody asks because it sounds impolite, and it is the only one that measures anything. Two percent of a fund is not a fact about conviction. Two percent of a fund as a proportion of the committing partner’s liquid net worth is. A manager for whom the commitment is a tenth of what he has outside the firm is exposed. A manager for whom it is a hundredth is making a gesture. A gesture is a fine thing, so long as it is not written into a document as an assurance.</p>
        <p class="mb-6">Behind all three questions is one observation about how the sentence works. It takes a threshold set by a tax authority and keeps the number after the threshold is retired. It attaches a phrase about alignment that the number never earned. It lets the amount be funded by the very people it claims to protect, and it passes the real cost to the people with the least say. Then it reads the total aloud as a virtue in a room where nobody has done the division. Every part of that is disclosed somewhere. None of it is said.</p>
        <p>The General Partner will commit two percent of aggregate Commitments to the Fund, in order to ensure an alignment of interests. Read it once more, slowly, as a piece of writing. It is a compliance floor from 1989 with a moral sentence bolted to the front of it. It is still in use, in every deck and every first meeting, in front of an investor who is about to sign for a hundred times as much and who has been trained by the sentence not to ask the only questions that would tell him what it means.</p>
      </div>
